  "publicacion": {
    "titulo": "Buffett's stock trailed the market by 40 points. Then this happened",
    "descripcion": "In 1999, Berkshire Hathaway's stock fell 19.9% while the S&P 500 (with dividends) gained 21.0%, a gap of about 40 points. In 2000 the tech bubble burst: Berkshire rose 26.6% while the S&P 500 fell 9.1%. 1999 wasn't even its worst year: in 1974 it lost 48.7%. From 1965 to 2024, Berkshire compounded 19.9% a year vs 10.4% for the S&P 500. All figures from Berkshire Hathaway's 2024 shareholder letter. But Berkshire is one survivor: many stocks fall and never recover, and past returns don't predict future ones. Not financial advice.",
